  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/2/2c/X-Men_Vol_1_2.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The first 3 pages are spent showing the various X-Men making their way to the School for Gifted Youngsters (apparently from New Haven, CT) after receiving a mental summons from Professor X, who then shows them a mental image of the \"Vanisher\" who robs a bank and then vanishes.&nbsp; He suspects he's a mutant like them and proceeds to have them step into \"The Danger Room,\" where they practice their skills until the middle of page 10.\n\nBut while the Professor tests his students...\n\n\"Meanwhile,\" the Vanisher appears inside the Pentagon and proceeds to proclaim that he is indeed a mutant, even using the \"Homo Superior\" lingo.&nbsp; He is only there to gloat, declaring that he will be returning \"within a few days\" to steal some secret defense plans.&nbsp; (He is already recognized by one of the men inside the Pentagon.)\n\nAfter the newspapers cover the story, we next the Vanisher in his lair, now surrounded by low-level crooks, who seem happy to become his lackeys (<i>in a rather corny scene which explicitly states that there has been enough time for his exploits to have been published in newspapers</i>).\n\n\"Meanwhile,\" back in the Danger Room, the lesson continues until a pre-appointed time, where we learn that he has an agreement with FBI Agent Fred Duncan.&nbsp; It's time to mentally contact him with the help of a \"psionic headband.\"&nbsp; It's apparently been a \"few days\" now, and the people in Washington DC are expecting the Vanisher's attack at any moment.\n\nThe X-Men immediately head off to Washington DC, on board a \"military air taxi.\"&nbsp; Sure enough, the Vanisher strikes at the Pentagon, stealing the plans, only to be confronted by the X-Men.&nbsp; They engage, during which time Angel tells Jean to use \"Maneuver eighteen!\"&nbsp; During the fight, the Vanisher seems surprised to learn that the X-Men are mutants also.&nbsp; He gasses Jean and vanishes with the plans.&nbsp; The newspapers report their failure the next day \"X-MEN FAIL IN ATTEMPT TO HALT THEFT!\"\n\nThe Vanisher then demands 10 million dollars to not turn over the plans to the Communists.&nbsp; People everywhere are calling the X-Men a bunch of phonies.&nbsp; Professor X now has to step in to help his team.&nbsp; The next day outside the White House, the X-Men and Vanisher (with his gang of lackeys) face off.&nbsp; He's come to collect his 10 million.&nbsp; But the Professor is now with them, and after a single creepy stare, the Vanisher suddenly cannot control his ability, and he's even forgotten who he is!\n\nWith the Vanisher subdued, the X-Men make quick work of the flunkies, and all is well again!",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME -- write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME -- write something",
  "review_text": "Similar to my <i>Avengers</i> 2 ([LINK:1963:AVEN_2|AVEN #2]) review, this was one so fraught with problems from the very start that writing a Plot Summary became a problem right away.&nbsp; As before, rather than litter it with [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=\"What the--?\"] reactions, I instead present a list of inconsistencies here:\n\n[HANG0] * The splash page shows the Beast wearing gloves (and then never again).&nbsp; The Beast <i>never</i> wears gloves, as you can see in the first issue (and indeed, even on page 2 of <i>this very issue</i>).&nbsp; This blunder was included in the <i>Marvel No-Prize</i> book published in the 1980s.\n\n[HANG0] * The X-Men are shown rushing to get to Westchester County and back to the School for Gifted Youngsters.&nbsp; The Beast even indicates that he's in New Haven (which is in Connecticut) and catches the \"commuter special\" to make his way.&nbsp; He even implies that they <i>all</i> were in New Haven.\n[BR]\n[BR] But... [SPAN: severity=warning; text=why is it that any of them had to make their way to the school at all?]&nbsp; The first issue seemed to imply that they were supposed to <i>attend</i> the boarding school, so wouldn't they always be on hand?\n\n[HANG0] * The girls who mob the Angel on page 2 seem to already know who he is, which <i>alone</i> presents a problem.&nbsp; And when Jean mentally moves the girls aside, the Angel warns her not to strain her \"[SPAN: severity=error; text=teleportation] powers\" (odd, considering that she has [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=italic; text=telekinetic]<i> </i>powers).&nbsp; (The error is repeated on page 7.)\n\n[HANG0] * Cyclops is <i>also</i> recognized by workers that he saves from a falling brick wall.&nbsp; Curious that people seem aware of the X-Men already.&nbsp; He and Iceman catch a ride on an ice cream truck.\n\n[HANG0] *Page 10 has the Vanisher using the same \"Homo Superior\" terminology as Magneto did in the first issue.&nbsp; Is this simply a coincidence, or did Magneto publish a research paper on the topic?\n\nThe Vanisher appears inside the Pentagon and announces that he will return within \"a few days\" to steal some defense plans...\n[ASSET: center | StealYourPlans | width=67%]\n\nThe very next panel then shows him at his secret lair, surrounded by low-level crooks.&nbsp; The narration explicitly states that this happens \"later, after reading of his incredible exploits in the newspapers,\" which means that sufficient time has gone by to make this <i>possible</i>.\n[ASSET: center | HiddenLair | width=67%]\nThe Vanisher actually <i>tells</i> these crooks that they shall become his \"lackeys.\"\n[BR] And their response is to say \"Three cheers for the <i>Vanisher</i>!\"\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(<i>Wow!</i>  This gives a whole new <i>meaning</i> to \"low-level crooks!\")]\n\nBut despite the fact that the newspapers have already published the story of the Vanisher's exploits at the Pentagon, Professor X has not ceased his training of the X-Men, because they're still in the Danger Room (see page 12, where the term \"Meanwhile\" is used in the narrative)!\n\n[ASSET: left | ItsAlmostTime | width=12%] When he finally <i>does</i> end the session and mentally reaches out to Fred Duncan to learn about the latest Vanisher developments, we learn that the people in Washington DC \"are expecting his attack at any moment!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] This makes it clear that the \"few days\" are now up.\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And it hints at the elapsed time of the Danger Room session that began on page 7...&nbsp; <b><i>Whoa!</i></b>)]\n\n[HANG0] * Page 13 shows the X-Men aboard a \"military air taxi\" in order to fly to Washington DC.&nbsp; But on page 12 of the first issue ([LINK:1963:XMEN_1|XMEN #1]), they boarded a \"remote-control private jet\" to fly all the way to Florida and face off against Magneto (and return on it too, as shown on its final page).&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=Why did they suddenly need the military air taxi if they already had a private jet at their disposal?]\n\n[ASSET: right | WhyNotUseASafe | width=12%] I actually laughed when I reached page 14 and saw the \"important continental defense plans\" sitting on a desk while surrounded by four soldiers with guns.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=<i>Oh sure</i>!&nbsp; Just lay the plans out in plain view, why don't you?]\n[BR]\n[BR] Did <i>no one </i>bother to ask if a <b><i>locked safe</i></b><i> </i>might have been a better choice?&nbsp; <i>Hahahaha!</i>\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(Our national security at work!)]\n\n[HANG0] * The Vanisher recognizes the X-Men at the bottom of page 14, but page 16 implies that he's unaware they are mutants like himself.\n\n[HANG0] * The Professor shows his students some footage he says was taken by a slow-motion camera capable of \"20,000 frames a second\" (page 19).&nbsp; But as this article notes: [CITATION: https://petapixel.com/2013/08/20/blast-from-the-past-18000fps-high-speed-photography-in-the-1960s], the maximum speed available at the time was 18,000fps.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(<i>Yes, I </i><b>know</b> it's a comic book, but I thought that would be fun to toss that in there!)]\n[BR]\n[BR] But more importantly, <i>none</i> of this information proves necessary to the plot.&nbsp; The footage is shown, shared with the folks at the While House for them to intently study, but when the final confrontation takes place, the Professor merely assaults the Vanisher mentally, making him forget who he is and how to use his powers.\n[BR]\n[BR] So what was the point of the footage in the first place?\n\nThe last couple of pages show the X-Men easily taking down the low-level crooks.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nA disappointing second issue with a villain I would be quite happy never to see again!\n\nWow, this was rather poor.&nbsp; Many pages wasted rushing to get to the School and them fighting amongst themselves with a story that was largely uninteresting.&nbsp; No information was provided about the Vanisher's past -- it's like he never had one before this issue.\n\n\nI complained that [LINK:1963:AVEN_2|AVEN #2] had a timing problem, in that the entire episode can't have taken place in more than an hour or so.&nbsp; In <i>this</i> issue, we have the <i>opposite</i> problem, where it seems the Professor's Danger Room session (that spans pages 7-12) takes place over an elapsed time of a \"few days.\"\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=<i>Boy</i>, that Professor is downright <b>brutal</b>!]\n\nObviously, the co-creators once again lost track of time, making this a [SPAN: severity=error; text=blatant error], and <i>clearly</i> not what was intended.&nbsp; It could have been easily corrected by reversing just a couple of scenes.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=Oh well...]\n\n[ASSET: left | DangerRoom | width=12%] On the positive side, the establishment of the Danger Room at least gives the X-Men a place where they can showcase the use of their powers.&nbsp; While it's a nice idea (especially in a \"school\" environment), you don't see the Fantastic Four doing anything of the sort (but perhaps they <i>should</i>).&nbsp; But more to the point, it provides Lee and Kirby with a built-in way to contribute nothing more to the plot than using these training exercises as a way to pad the page count.\n\nIn the end, the Professor shows up and takes care of the problem single-handedly.\n[BR] This leads to a couple of very obvious questions:\n[HANG] * Why did he even need the X-Men if he could have dealt with the Vanisher by himself all <i>along</i>?\n[HANG] * Was his physical presence actually required?&nbsp; Is there perhaps some sort of distance limitation to his powers?\n\n[ASSET: right | CreepyLook | width=12%] The answer to the first question is likely provided on page 18, where the Professor indicates to them that he was letting them get experience.\n[BR]\n[BR] And if for no other reason than Jack Kirby got to draw this [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=downright creepy look] on the face of the Professor, I guess it's okay that he showed up in person!&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(<i>Holy cow!</i>)]",
  "ownership": "I did own a VG copy of this book years ago, which I bought on Feb 19, 2000 for $183.75 (but not part of the \"2000 collection\"), but it was sold that same year, <i>well</i> before my full 2018 inventory, so I did not count it as being \"owned\" anymore.&nbsp; I have no record of how much I got for it.",
